WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.740 --> 00:00:03.450
- [Narrator] This is
NASA's Perseverance rover.

00:00:03.450 --> 00:00:05.690
And this is China's Zhurong Rover,

00:00:05.690 --> 00:00:08.130
named after the Chinese god of fire.

00:00:08.130 --> 00:00:09.230
And these two vehicles

00:00:09.230 --> 00:00:11.400
are on a mission to explore Mars.

00:00:11.400 --> 00:00:12.920
- The US and China are clearly

00:00:12.920 --> 00:00:15.036
in a political competition here on Earth,

00:00:15.036 --> 00:00:16.730
so it should not be surprising

00:00:16.730 --> 00:00:21.124
that it has now also become
part of the space aspect.

00:00:21.124 --> 00:00:23.137
- [Announcer] Roger, three seconds.

00:00:23.137 --> 00:00:24.700
- [Narrator] The US is the global leader

00:00:24.700 --> 00:00:26.920
with more than 50 years of Mars research.

00:00:26.920 --> 00:00:28.750
- [NASA] About 20 meters off the surface.

00:00:28.750 --> 00:00:29.990
- [Narrator] And last February,

00:00:29.990 --> 00:00:33.200
Perseverance was NASA's
fifth rover to land there.

00:00:33.200 --> 00:00:35.420
China is the newcomer with a Mars program

00:00:35.420 --> 00:00:37.570
that started in 2006.

00:00:37.570 --> 00:00:39.130
But the country has big plans

00:00:39.130 --> 00:00:41.697
for its first mission, called Tianwen-1.

00:00:41.697 --> 00:00:43.261
(people cheering)

00:00:43.261 --> 00:00:46.690
- The fact that Tianwen-1
is an orbiter lander

00:00:46.690 --> 00:00:48.230
and rover all in one

00:00:48.230 --> 00:00:51.490
is a very gutsy move for a first mission.

00:00:51.490 --> 00:00:53.590
- [Narrator] This ambition makes
the Zhurong rover different

00:00:53.590 --> 00:00:55.330
from NASA's Perseverance.

00:00:55.330 --> 00:00:56.890
So here's what these two vehicles

00:00:56.890 --> 00:00:58.600
are carrying and what they can tell us

00:00:58.600 --> 00:01:01.513
about the new space race
between both superpowers.

00:01:03.010 --> 00:01:04.730
Since this is China's first attempt

00:01:04.730 --> 00:01:06.070
to touch down on Mars,

00:01:06.070 --> 00:01:07.930
a major goal is to make sure its rover

00:01:07.930 --> 00:01:09.850
actually lands in one piece.

00:01:09.850 --> 00:01:12.150
And it's expected to
happen some time in May.

00:01:12.150 --> 00:01:14.553
- Landing on Mars is very difficult.

00:01:14.553 --> 00:01:17.030
- [Narrator] Jonathan
McDowell is an astrophysicist

00:01:17.030 --> 00:01:19.920
at the Harvard-Smithsonian
Center for Astrophysics.

00:01:19.920 --> 00:01:21.570
And he's been studying space programs

00:01:21.570 --> 00:01:23.720
around the world for over 40 years.

00:01:23.720 --> 00:01:26.890
- It's harder than landing
on Earth from space

00:01:26.890 --> 00:01:29.570
because the atmosphere is thinner,

00:01:29.570 --> 00:01:32.410
so parachutes don't work as well.

00:01:32.410 --> 00:01:34.010
And it's harder than landing on the moon.

00:01:34.010 --> 00:01:36.560
You can't just use the rocket engines.

00:01:36.560 --> 00:01:37.610
- [Narrator] So the Chinese lander

00:01:37.610 --> 00:01:39.460
that will release the Zhurong rover

00:01:39.460 --> 00:01:41.250
has an intermediate solution,

00:01:41.250 --> 00:01:43.160
using parachutes and rocket thrusters

00:01:43.160 --> 00:01:45.320
to slow down for a softer landing.

00:01:45.320 --> 00:01:49.450
- You wanna go somewhere that
you maximize your chances

00:01:49.450 --> 00:01:50.480
of a successful mission

00:01:50.480 --> 00:01:52.150
because there's already so many things

00:01:52.150 --> 00:01:53.370
that can go wrong.

00:01:53.370 --> 00:01:55.730
- [Narrator] So China has
chosen a relatively easy place

00:01:55.730 --> 00:01:58.540
for touchdown, a basin in
the northern hemisphere,

00:01:58.540 --> 00:02:00.580
known as Utopia Planitia.

00:02:00.580 --> 00:02:02.800
It's where NASA landed
its second Mars lander,

00:02:02.800 --> 00:02:05.570
the Viking 2, in 1976.

00:02:05.570 --> 00:02:07.940
- It doesn't have a lot of big mountains

00:02:07.940 --> 00:02:10.410
or big canyons or cliffs

00:02:10.410 --> 00:02:12.240
that the rover might fall off

00:02:12.240 --> 00:02:17.240
and so it's a good first
place to try and explore.

00:02:17.520 --> 00:02:19.470
- [Narrator] Since this
is old terrain for NASA,

00:02:19.470 --> 00:02:21.480
Perseverance landed in a different area,

00:02:21.480 --> 00:02:23.180
called the Jezero Crater.

00:02:23.180 --> 00:02:25.550
- [Jonathan] So Perseverance has landed

00:02:25.550 --> 00:02:28.560
at the edge of an old river delta.

00:02:28.560 --> 00:02:30.040
So you have this landscape,

00:02:30.040 --> 00:02:32.110
which is a mixture of craters

00:02:32.110 --> 00:02:35.780
and mountains that are
gentle, rolling hills.

00:02:35.780 --> 00:02:37.420
- [Narrator] It's a tougher
landing area compared

00:02:37.420 --> 00:02:39.097
with Utopia Planitia.

00:02:39.097 --> 00:02:40.380
But Perseverance is equipped

00:02:40.380 --> 00:02:43.731
with a more advanced landing
system called Skycrane.

00:02:43.731 --> 00:02:45.570
- [NASA] About to conduct
the Skycrane maneuver.

00:02:45.570 --> 00:02:48.000
- [Narrator] So it helped
the rover hover above ground

00:02:48.000 --> 00:02:50.793
while a computer vision
system scanned for obstacles.

00:02:51.760 --> 00:02:53.490
These two different types of terrains

00:02:53.490 --> 00:02:55.440
mean both countries
have packed their rovers

00:02:55.440 --> 00:02:58.080
with different scientific goals in mind.

00:02:58.080 --> 00:02:59.650
And one of China's main focuses

00:02:59.650 --> 00:03:02.180
is getting familiar with the new planet.

00:03:02.180 --> 00:03:05.580
- It has a pretty standard
set of instruments

00:03:05.580 --> 00:03:08.370
that will help Chinese
scientists understand

00:03:08.370 --> 00:03:10.630
what it's like to operate at Mars.

00:03:10.630 --> 00:03:12.200
- [Narrator] China is bringing better tech

00:03:12.200 --> 00:03:15.460
to Utopia Planitia than
when NASA did decades ago.

00:03:15.460 --> 00:03:17.310
- The technology on the Zhurong rover

00:03:18.440 --> 00:03:22.330
is probably similar to
what was on the Spirit

00:03:22.330 --> 00:03:25.510
and Opportunity rovers that
NASA flew a few years ago.

00:03:25.510 --> 00:03:28.210
And it's still fairly modern tech.

00:03:28.210 --> 00:03:30.660
- [Narrator] So this is
Zhurong's multi-spectral camera,

00:03:30.660 --> 00:03:32.920
which can take pictures in
different colored filters

00:03:32.920 --> 00:03:35.770
to study various rocks on Mars' surface.

00:03:35.770 --> 00:03:37.290
There's also a penetrating radar

00:03:37.290 --> 00:03:38.780
that emits radio waves

00:03:38.780 --> 00:03:40.078
and when pointed at the surface,

00:03:40.078 --> 00:03:41.920
the rover can pick up signals

00:03:41.920 --> 00:03:44.200
and collect data about what's underneath,

00:03:44.200 --> 00:03:45.893
particularly water ice.

00:03:48.180 --> 00:03:51.200
Like Zhurong, Perseverance
is also surveying Mars

00:03:51.200 --> 00:03:52.890
but it's larger in size.

00:03:52.890 --> 00:03:55.210
So NASA has equipped it with more tools,

00:03:55.210 --> 00:03:57.100
like a helicopter called Ingenuity

00:03:57.100 --> 00:03:59.070
that could one day help explore the planet

00:03:59.070 --> 00:04:00.480
on a bigger scale.

00:04:00.480 --> 00:04:02.450
- [Jonathan] It's the first object

00:04:02.450 --> 00:04:04.170
to make a controlled flight

00:04:04.170 --> 00:04:06.190
in the atmosphere of another planet,

00:04:06.190 --> 00:04:08.366
which is quite an engineering challenge.

00:04:08.366 --> 00:04:11.650
(NASA cheering)

00:04:11.650 --> 00:04:12.740
- [Narrator] One of the main goals

00:04:12.740 --> 00:04:14.980
is to learn about life on the red planet

00:04:14.980 --> 00:04:16.610
and scientists say the best way

00:04:16.610 --> 00:04:19.300
to do this is bringing
back samples to Earth.

00:04:19.300 --> 00:04:21.110
So Perseverance has a robotic arm

00:04:21.110 --> 00:04:22.560
that can drill into the surface,

00:04:22.560 --> 00:04:25.380
extract rocks or collect loose soil.

00:04:25.380 --> 00:04:27.330
The samples are then put in clean tubes

00:04:27.330 --> 00:04:28.873
to prevent contamination.

00:04:30.620 --> 00:04:32.240
And then these are stored here,

00:04:32.240 --> 00:04:34.118
inside the rover's tummy.

00:04:34.118 --> 00:04:36.050
NASA plans to bring back the samples

00:04:36.050 --> 00:04:38.350
to Earth by 2031 in a joint mission

00:04:38.350 --> 00:04:40.263
with the European Space Agency.

00:04:41.110 --> 00:04:42.560
- Even though, in some respects,

00:04:42.560 --> 00:04:45.500
the US space technology is
still the most advanced,

00:04:45.500 --> 00:04:48.265
the missions that China's executing

00:04:48.265 --> 00:04:50.760
are actually very impressive now.

00:04:50.760 --> 00:04:53.180
So they've been catching up very quickly.

00:04:53.180 --> 00:04:55.590
- China's space program
has enjoyed support

00:04:55.590 --> 00:04:57.260
from the absolute top leadership

00:04:57.260 --> 00:05:00.970
since the very beginning,
dating back to Mao Zedong.

00:05:00.970 --> 00:05:02.380
- [Narrator] Dean Cheng
is a research fellow

00:05:02.380 --> 00:05:03.870
at the Washington-based think tank,

00:05:03.870 --> 00:05:05.430
the Heritage Foundation.

00:05:05.430 --> 00:05:07.290
And he's been watching
China's space program

00:05:07.290 --> 00:05:08.980
for more than 20 years.

00:05:08.980 --> 00:05:10.770
He says the country's political system

00:05:10.770 --> 00:05:12.010
is an advantage when it comes

00:05:12.010 --> 00:05:14.990
to planning ambitious
projects, like going to Mars.

00:05:14.990 --> 00:05:17.160
- Xi Jinping and the
Chinese Communist Party

00:05:17.160 --> 00:05:19.520
doesn't face regular elections.

00:05:19.520 --> 00:05:24.520
So China is able to make
longer-term decisions

00:05:25.140 --> 00:05:27.453
and as important, sustain those decisions.

00:05:29.110 --> 00:05:31.397
- [Narrator] And those decisions
are financially sustained.

00:05:31.397 --> 00:05:33.680
So while there's little public information

00:05:33.680 --> 00:05:35.360
about China's space budget,

00:05:35.360 --> 00:05:38.030
Cheng says it's likely not a small amount.

00:05:38.030 --> 00:05:39.630
- China doesn't provide those numbers

00:05:39.630 --> 00:05:41.070
but what we do know is, for example,

00:05:41.070 --> 00:05:44.210
China has four space launch facilities.

00:05:44.210 --> 00:05:46.230
That's more than any other country.

00:05:46.230 --> 00:05:47.350
- [Narrator] China's state-funded program

00:05:47.350 --> 00:05:48.770
makes it fundamentally different

00:05:48.770 --> 00:05:50.287
to how NASA works.

00:05:50.287 --> 00:05:52.040
(NASA cheering)

00:05:52.040 --> 00:05:53.960
- So the US, for example,

00:05:53.960 --> 00:05:55.610
has talked about going back to the moon

00:05:55.610 --> 00:05:57.420
for at least 20 years.

00:05:57.420 --> 00:06:00.850
But with each Congress
and with each president,

00:06:00.850 --> 00:06:04.140
priorities change,
funding therefore changes.

00:06:04.140 --> 00:06:06.410
- [Narrator] For instance,
NASA's annual budget

00:06:06.410 --> 00:06:08.810
hasn't hit its peak from the 1960s,

00:06:08.810 --> 00:06:11.490
fluctuating more or less over the years.

00:06:11.490 --> 00:06:13.040
And in contrast to China,

00:06:13.040 --> 00:06:15.720
NASA is an open civilian agency.

00:06:15.720 --> 00:06:17.040
It has shared its technology

00:06:17.040 --> 00:06:19.597
and fostered a commercial space industry.

00:06:19.597 --> 00:06:20.446
(rocket roaring)

00:06:20.446 --> 00:06:21.430
- [Announcer] And liftoff.

00:06:21.430 --> 00:06:22.600
- [Narrator] Which has taken over many

00:06:22.600 --> 00:06:25.823
of NASA's launch tasks and
helped with space exploration.

00:06:27.460 --> 00:06:30.240
So if the Zhurong lands
and operates on Mars,

00:06:30.240 --> 00:06:31.940
that means China will have done more

00:06:31.940 --> 00:06:34.930
and faster in less time than the US.

00:06:34.930 --> 00:06:36.610
This success wouldn't just be a point

00:06:36.610 --> 00:06:37.806
of national pride

00:06:37.806 --> 00:06:40.130
but would also get the country closer

00:06:40.130 --> 00:06:42.290
to accomplishing other space goals,

00:06:42.290 --> 00:06:43.810
including building a moon base

00:06:43.810 --> 00:06:46.290
and its own International Space Station.

00:06:46.290 --> 00:06:49.000
- China is presenting
itself as an alternative.

00:06:49.000 --> 00:06:51.170
With regards to the moon and Mars,

00:06:51.170 --> 00:06:54.109
most of that is for political signaling,

00:06:54.109 --> 00:06:56.970
political demonstrations to show the world

00:06:56.970 --> 00:06:59.840
who has a more capable technological base

00:06:59.840 --> 00:07:02.174
and a better political system.

00:07:02.174 --> 00:07:04.924
(dramatic music)

